;; A yield point inside a body-local function.
;;
;; `helper` is written inside a bjoroutine and never leaves it, which makes it
;; look like part of the same body. It is not: a body-local `(defun ...)` is
;; emitted as a C# local function, and a local function inside an async method
;; is not itself async. Only the loop shares its enclosing member, and only when
;; `LoopLowering` can promote it.
;;
;; `helper` is called twice on purpose. Called once, beta reduction would
;; substitute it away and the yield point would land in the bjoroutine's own
;; body, where it is perfectly legal — so a single call tests nothing.
;;
;; What this pins is that the message names `helper`. The construct in the way
;; has a name, the reader gave it that name, and reporting an anonymous
;; `(fun ...)` would send them looking for a lambda they never wrote.

;; EXPECT-ERROR: calling 'fetch' is a yield point
;; EXPECT-ERROR: yield point is not allowed here
;; EXPECT-ERROR: inside 'helper', a body-local function
;; EXPECT-ERROR: a local function inside an async method is not itself async

(: fetch (-> int int))
(defbjo (fetch x) (* x 2))

(: outer (-> int int))
(defbjo (outer n)
  (defun (helper y) (fetch y))
  (+ (helper n) (helper (+ n 1))))

(defun (main args) 0)
